{%- comment -%}
  Virtual Try-On button.
  Accepts:
  - product: {Object} product object
  - block: {Object} block object (only when placement is 'block')
  - placement: {String} 'sticky' (default), 'block', 'tile' or 'gallery'
  - stacked: {Boolean} gallery placement only — true when the rating pill is
             also rendering, so the try-on pill lifts clear of it

  Placement:
    'sticky'  — rendered inside the permanent sticky buy bar in
                sections/main-product.liquid.
    'block'   — the original theme-editor block. No product template references
                it any more, but the markup is kept so the block can be dropped
                back into a template without rebuilding the data attributes.
    'tile'    — the buy bar's 48px thumbnail, which IS the minimised try-on.
    'gallery' — a glass pill in the PDP gallery's bottom-right corner, directly
                above the rating pill. The try-on's first-contact surface: it
                sits on the hero image, where the shopper is already looking,
                instead of waiting for them to reach the buy bar.

  All four carry the SAME data contract. assets/vto.js opens from a delegated
  [data-vto-trigger] click and reads the attributes off whatever element it
  finds, so a new placement needs markup and nothing else. 'tile' and 'gallery'
  additionally carry data-vto-tile, which enrols them in the progress state
  machine — both are dressed together, from one session, by construction.

  Image resolution (v3 — merchant-selectable):
    front: custom.vto_front_image metafield → product.images[0] → featured_image
    back:  custom.vto_back_image  metafield → product.images[1] (optional; back tab hides if missing)
  Merchants pick the exact garment images per product in the admin
  (Settings → Custom data → Products: "VTO Front/Back Image"). When left
  blank, behaviour falls back to the original first/second-image logic.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- if true -%}
{%- assign vto_placement = placement | default: 'sticky' -%}
{%- assign default_variant_id = product.selected_or_first_available_variant.id -%}

{%- comment -%}
  ── Reference image selection ────────────────────────────────────────────
  The merchant metafields win when set. They are set on 38 of 92 products;
  the entire 48-piece polo range has neither, so the fallback below is what
  actually runs on the flagship catalogue and has to be good.

  The old fallback was positional — images[0] for front, images[1] for back —
  and images[1] is the ON-MODEL lifestyle shot on every polo:

      images[0]  "… — front"      -1-front.png       flat garment
      images[1]  "… — on model"   -2-on-model.png    a different human
      images[2]  "… — back"       -3-back.png        flat garment, back

  So every polo sent a photograph of a model as the "back of the garment".
  That is the exact hazard the model is most prone to: with a weak customer
  photo it reproduces the person in the reference instead of the customer,
  and the real back design was never in the request at all.

  Selection is now by meaning rather than position: match the alt text and
  the filename, and explicitly exclude on-model, lifestyle and detail crops
  from BOTH slots. Shopify's alts here are structured ("— front", "— back",
  "— on model", "— detail"), and the filenames carry the same words, so
  either signal alone is enough.

  When nothing matches, front degrades to the first non-excluded image and
  back is left EMPTY on purpose. A missing back view costs the shopper one
  of two images; a wrong back view costs them a picture of a stranger, and
  the backend already treats back as optional.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign front_image = product.metafields.custom.vto_front_image.value -%}
{%- assign back_image = product.metafields.custom.vto_back_image.value -%}

{%- if front_image == blank or back_image == blank -%}
  {%- assign vto_first_clean = blank -%}
  {%- for vto_img in product.images -%}
    {%- assign vto_tag = vto_img.alt | default: '' | downcase -%}
    {%- assign vto_src = vto_img | image_url: width: 1 | downcase -%}
    {%- assign vto_tag = vto_tag | append: ' ' | append: vto_src -%}

    {%- assign vto_skip = false -%}
    {%- if vto_tag contains 'on-model' or vto_tag contains 'on model' or vto_tag contains 'onmodel' -%}
      {%- assign vto_skip = true -%}
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- if vto_tag contains 'lifestyle' or vto_tag contains 'detail' or vto_tag contains 'campaign' -%}
      {%- assign vto_skip = true -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- unless vto_skip -%}
      {%- if vto_first_clean == blank -%}
        {%- assign vto_first_clean = vto_img -%}
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if front_image == blank and vto_tag contains 'front' -%}
        {%- assign front_image = vto_img -%}
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if back_image == blank and vto_tag contains 'back' -%}
        {%- assign back_image = vto_img -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endunless -%}
  {%- endfor -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Front is required — without it there is no try-on at all — so it falls
    back to the first image that is not a model or a crop, and finally to the
    featured image. Back deliberately has no such fallback.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if front_image == blank -%}
    {%- assign front_image = vto_first_clean | default: product.featured_image -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  ── Fit context ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  custom.vto_fit_hint has no metafield definition on this store and is unset
  on all 92 products, so this path was permanently inert and the model never
  received any cut guidance.

  The size-chart registry already carries a written fit line per garment
  ("Regular fit — cut close to the body, not oversized.") plus the designed
  ease, for every chart. That is better material than a three-value enum and
  it is already maintained. The chart key travels on the trigger as
  data-sc-chart; assets/vto.js resolves the line from the registry and sends
  it. The metafield still wins when a merchant sets it.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign fit_hint_raw = product.metafields.custom.vto_fit_hint.value | default: '' | downcase | strip -%}
{%- assign fit_hint = '' -%}
{%- if fit_hint_raw == 'oversized' or fit_hint_raw == 'fitted' or fit_hint_raw == 'relaxed' -%}
  {%- assign fit_hint = fit_hint_raw -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%} Which option position is "Size" (matches the theme's variant picker logic) so VTO can label size pills. -1 when there's no size option. {%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign vto_size_index = -1 -%}
{%- assign vto_size_trigger = 'products.general.size_trigger' | t | downcase -%}
{%- if vto_size_trigger != blank -%}
  {%- for opt in product.options_with_values -%}
    {%- assign opt_name_dc = opt.name | downcase -%}
    {%- if opt_name_dc contains vto_size_trigger -%}
      {%- assign vto_size_index = forloop.index0 -%}
      {%- break -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endfor -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Only the two text-button placements wear any of this, and a product page
  renders more than one placement. Emitting it unconditionally put a second
  identical copy of the whole block inside the gallery's badge layer.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if vto_placement == 'sticky' or vto_placement == 'block' -%}
<style>
  .vto-button-wrapper {
    margin-top: 12px;
    min-height: 46px;
  }
  .vto-trigger-btn {
    position: relative;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 8px;
    width: 100%;
    padding: 13px 24px;
    background: #5c1011;
    color: #ffffff;
    border: none;
    border-radius: 10px;
    font-family: inherit;
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.2px;
    cursor: pointer;
    box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(92,16,17,0.25);
    box-sizing: border-box;
    min-height: 46px;
    transition: background 0.18s ease, transform 0.15s ease, box-shadow 0.18s ease;
  }
  .vto-trigger-btn__icon {
    width: 18px;
    height: 18px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }
  .vto-trigger-btn:hover {
    background: #7a1a1b;
    transform: translateY(-1px);
    box-shadow: 0 4px 14px rgba(92,16,17,0.3);
  }
  .vto-trigger-btn:active {
    transform: translateY(0) scale(0.985);
    box-shadow: none;
  }
  .vto-trigger-btn__badge {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 3px;
    padding: 2px 7px;
    margin-left: 4px;
    border-radius: 99px;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.22);
    font-size: 10px;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.5px;
    color: #fff;
  }
  .vto-trigger-btn__badge svg {
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  {%- comment -%}
    Sticky-bar variant. Sits directly above .sticky-buy__actions, so it drops
    the wrapper's top margin and matches that row's radius and metrics rather
    than the product-info block's.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  .vto-trigger-btn--sticky {
    margin: 0 0 8px;
    border-radius: 12px;
    padding: 12px 16px;
    min-height: 44px;
    font-size: 13px;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    box-shadow: none;
  }
  .vto-trigger-btn--sticky:hover {
    transform: none;
    box-shadow: none;
  }
  @media (max-width: 380px) {
    .vto-trigger-btn--sticky {
      font-size: 12px;
      padding: 11px 12px;
    }
  }
</style>
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  'tile' is the buy bar's 48px thumbnail. It carries the identical data
  contract as the other placements — vto.js opens from a delegated
  [data-vto-trigger] click and reads these attributes off the element it
  finds — so the contract stays defined in exactly one place. Only the
  class and the inner markup differ; onrepeat-buy-bar.liquid styles it.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if vto_placement == 'block' -%}<div class="vto-button-wrapper" {{ block.shopify_attributes }}>{%- endif -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    No whitespace-stripping tags inside this attribute list — the hyphen form.
    Whitespace is the only thing separating one attribute from the next, and
    stripping it welds them together.

    The tile branch used to end on the bare attribute data-vto-tile, and the
    stripped endif that closed the branch pulled the next line up against it.
    The browser then tokenised

        data-vto-tile aria-label="Virtual Try-On for ..."

    as ONE attribute named data-vto-tilearia-label. So the tile carried neither
    data-vto-tile nor an accessible name: the selector .onrepeat-bb__thumb with
    [data-vto-tile] matched nothing on any page, every minimised try-on fell
    through to the floating copy this rebuild deleted, and the buy bar's
    try-on button was unlabelled for screen readers. The other two placements
    escaped only because their branch ends in a QUOTED value, where a missing
    space is a recoverable parse error rather than a fused name.

    Plain tags keep the newlines. The cost is some blank lines in the rendered
    HTML; the alternative is silent, invisible and expensive.

    (Tag syntax is spelled out in prose above on purpose: Liquid parses block
    tags inside a comment, so a literal endif written here would unbalance the
    template it is documenting.)
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <button
    type="button"
    {% if vto_placement == 'tile' %}
      {%- comment -%}
        vto-tile is the progress layer, styled in assets/vto.css. This tile IS
        the minimised try-on: a running or finished render is shown here, on
        the thumbnail the bar already displays, rather than on a second tile
        floating over the card. assets/vto.js swaps the image, lights the ring
        and hands the tile back untouched when the session ends — including
        when the session belongs to a DIFFERENT product, which is how a try-on
        follows the shopper from one product page to the next.
      {%- endcomment %}
      class="onrepeat-bb__thumb vto-tile"
      data-vto-tile
    {% elsif vto_placement == 'gallery' %}
      {%- comment -%}
        data-vto-tile-noimg tells the state machine not to swap a thumbnail in:
        this placement is an icon, and injecting the 1024px result into it would
        wreck the pill's layout. It still takes the ring, the label, the dot and
        the percentage — everything the state machine drives that is not a
        picture.

        The data-vto-label-* attributes override the tile's terse copy. The buy
        bar's label strip is 7.5px wide and can hold nothing longer than "View";
        this pill has the room to say what is actually happening — and "Paused"
        instead of "Creating" is the difference between an honest indicator and
        one still claiming to work after the connection dropped.
      {%- endcomment %}
      {%- comment -%}
        data-vto-hintable opts this placement into the one-time attention glow.
        A bare attribute for the same reason data-vto-tile is one: the hint is a
        property of a placement that has room to be noticed, not of a class, and
        the buy bar's 48px thumbnail deliberately does not have it — a glow on a
        control that is off-screen until you scroll is a glow nobody sees.
      {%- endcomment %}
      class="vto-gpill{% if stacked %} vto-gpill--stacked{% endif %}"
      data-vto-tile
      data-vto-tile-noimg
      data-vto-hintable
      data-vto-label-generating="Creating"
      data-vto-label-offline="Paused"
      data-vto-label-ready="View look"
    {% else %}
      class="vto-trigger-btn{% if vto_placement == 'sticky' %} vto-trigger-btn--sticky{% endif %}"
    {% endif %}
    aria-label="Virtual Try-On for {{ product.title | escape }}"
    aria-controls="VtoModal"
    aria-expanded="false"
    data-vto-trigger
    data-product-id="{{ product.id }}"
    data-product-title="{{ product.title | escape }}"
    data-sc-chart="{%- render 'size-chart-key', product: product -%}"
    data-product-url="{{ product.url }}"
    data-front-image="{{ front_image | image_url: width: 1024 }}"
    data-back-image="{% if back_image %}{{ back_image | image_url: width: 1024 }}{% endif %}"
    data-fit-hint="{{ fit_hint }}"
    data-default-variant-id="{{ default_variant_id }}"
    data-size-option-index="{{ vto_size_index }}"
    data-variants='{{ product.variants | json | escape }}'
  >
    {%- if vto_placement == 'tile' -%}
      {%- assign tile_media = product.selected_or_first_available_variant.featured_media | default: product.featured_media -%}
      {%- if tile_media != blank -%}
        {{- tile_media.preview_image | image_url: width: 144 | image_tag: loading: 'lazy', widths: '48,96,144', alt: '' -}}
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- render 'vto-tile-ring' -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        Stays aria-hidden: the button already carries a full aria-label, and
        vto.js rewrites THAT as progress changes. Exposing the 7.5px label as
        well would have a screen reader read the control's name twice.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <span data-vto-tile-label aria-hidden="true">Try on</span>
      <span class="vto-tile__dot" data-vto-tile-dot aria-hidden="true"></span>
    {%- elsif vto_placement == 'gallery' -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        The icon disc doubles as the progress ring — one 22px object that is the
        affordance at rest and the meter while working, rather than a button
        that grows a second thing when it becomes busy.

        pathLength="100" normalises the circumference to 100 units, which is
        what lets `stroke-dasharray: <pct> 100` in assets/vto-gallery-pill.css
        mean percent directly against the --vto-pct that vto.js writes. The same
        trick, and the same variable, as the buy bar tile's rounded-rect ring.

        Everything inside is aria-hidden: the button already carries a full
        accessible name and vto.js rewrites THAT as the state changes, so
        exposing the label and the percentage as well would have a screen reader
        read the control twice, the second time on every progress tick.
      {%- endcomment %}
      <span class="vto-gpill__disc" aria-hidden="true">
        <svg class="vto-gpill__ring" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" focusable="false">
          <circle class="vto-gpill__track" cx="12" cy="12" r="10.6"/>
          <circle class="vto-gpill__fill" cx="12" cy="12" r="10.6" pathLength="100"/>
        </svg>
        <svg class="vto-gpill__ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" focusable="false">
          <path d="M14.5 4h-5L7 7H4a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v9a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h16a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V9a2 2 0 0 0-2-2h-3l-2.5-3z"/>
          <circle cx="12" cy="13" r="3"/>
        </svg>
      </span>
      <span class="vto-gpill__lab" aria-hidden="true">
        <span data-vto-tile-label>Try on</span>
        <span class="vto-gpill__pct" data-vto-tile-pct></span>
        <span class="vto-gpill__ai">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor" focusable="false" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M12 2l2.1 6.26L20.7 8.7l-4.9 4.3 1.4 6.5L12 15.8 6.8 19.5l1.4-6.5-4.9-4.3 6.6-.44L12 2z"/>
          </svg>
          AI
        </span>
      </span>
      {%- comment -%}
        Badged on the PILL's corner, not the icon's. A notification dot tucked
        inside the pill reads as part of the camera glyph rather than as
        "something is waiting for you", which is the whole reason it exists.
      {%- endcomment %}
      <span class="vto-gpill__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    {%- else -%}
      <svg class="vto-trigger-btn__icon" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
        <path d="M14.5 4h-5L7 7H4a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v9a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h16a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V9a2 2 0 0 0-2-2h-3l-2.5-3z"/>
        <circle cx="12" cy="13" r="3"/>
      </svg>
      <span>Virtual Try-On</span>
      <span class="vto-trigger-btn__badge" aria-hidden="true">
        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="10" height="10" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor">
          <path d="M12 2L14.09 8.26L20.66 8.73L15.77 13.02L17.18 19.5L12 15.79L6.82 19.5L8.23 13.02L3.34 8.73L9.91 8.26L12 2Z"/>
        </svg>
        AI
      </span>
    {%- endif -%}
  </button>
{%- if vto_placement == 'block' -%}</div>{%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
